{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg6u2yRj6m8MDd2xEKRT9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Crawling** is about *discovering* pages. **Scraping** is about *extracting* data from them.\n\n| | Crawling | Scraping |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Goal** | Find and traverse URLs by following links | Pull specific data out of a page |\n| **Output** | A list or map of pages (URLs, plus raw content) | Structured data (prices, titles, emails, tables) |\n| **Scope** | Broad: many pages across a site or the web | Narrow: targeted fields on known pages |\n| **Typical tool** | Search-engine bots, Scrapy spiders, site mappers | BeautifulSoup, Playwright, CSS/XPath selectors |\n| **Example** | Googlebot indexing the web | Collecting product prices from an e-commerce site |\n\n**How they fit together:** They're often combined. A crawler walks a site and collects URLs. A scraper then parses each page for the fields you want. Many tools, such as Scrapy and Firecrawl, do both.\n\n**Other differences:**\n- **Crawling** has to handle deduplication, link depth, `robots.txt`, rate limiting, and sitemaps.\n- **Scraping** has to handle changing HTML structure, JavaScript-rendered content, and anti-bot measures.\n- **Legality and ethics** differ in emphasis. Crawling mostly raises politeness and load concerns. Scraping raises questions about terms of service, copyright, and personal data."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6956,"cache_read_input_tokens":11227,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6956},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7fa21459-86f4-4e07-a088-6b8d55dfe22e","uuid":"2b1901ed-c28f-45d0-9211-e7face1ea1a5","timesta